{
  "slug": "cracking-the-safe",
  "hints": [
    "Think of every length-(n-1) digit sequence as a node. What edge should represent a length-n password?",
    "If every password is an edge, the required string corresponds to a walk that uses every edge exactly once.",
    "An Eulerian circuit gives exactly that traversal; record the added digit of each edge.",
    "The shortest possible length follows from having k^n distinct length-n windows."
  ],
  "title": "Cracking the Safe Problem",
  "content": {
    "notes": [
      "A valid answer has length exactly k^n + n - 1 and contains every possible length-n password exactly once as a contiguous substring."
    ],
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 4",
      "1 <= k <= 10"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Single line: JSON array of all arguments, e.g. [arg1, arg2, ...]",
    "outputFormat": "Single line: JSON-serialised return value",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
