  "solution": {
    "notes": [
      "The construction uses every possible length-n password exactly once, so no shorter string can contain all k^n passwords.",
      "The canonical implementation uses a compact visited-edge array and an iterative Eulerian traversal."
    ],
    "approach": "Model each length-(n-1) digit string as a vertex in a de Bruijn directed graph. Each possible length-n password is an edge from its first n-1 digits to its last n-1 digits. An Eulerian circuit traverses every edge exactly once; recording each traversed edge digit and appending the initial n-1 digits produces a shortest unlocking sequence.",
    "editorial": {
      "better": {
        "complexity": "Time O(k^n), space O(k^n).",
        "description": "Build the de Bruijn graph explicitly and use DFS with a visited-edge structure to construct an Eulerian circuit. This removes the need to enumerate candidate passwords separately, while still using linear time in the number of edges."
      },
      "optimal": {
        "complexity": "Time O(k^n), space O(k^n).",
        "description": "Use an Eulerian-circuit construction on the de Bruijn graph. Each edge represents one possible password and is traversed exactly once. Appending the starting n-1 digits to the recorded edge labels gives a string of length k^n+n-1, which is minimal because that many length-n windows are needed to cover k^n distinct passwords."
      },
      "bruteForce": {
        "complexity": "Exponential in the candidate-string length; not suitable for the maximum constraints.",
        "description": "Try candidate digit strings in increasing length and check whether all k^n possible passwords occur as length-n substrings. This directly follows the definition but has exponential search and repeated substring checking, so it is impractical except for tiny inputs."
      }
    },
    "timeComplexity": "O(k^n)",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(k^n)",
    "code": {
      "C": "#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nstatic int ipow(int a, int b) {\n    int r = 1;\n    while (b-- > 0) r *= a;\n    return r;\n}\n\nchar* crackSafe(int n, int k) {\n    int m = ipow(k, n - 1);\n    int total = m * k;\n    int* p = (int*)malloc((size_t)total * sizeof(int));\n    char* result = (char*)malloc((size_t)(total + n) * sizeof(char));\n    int pos = 0;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)\n        for (int q = 0; q < m; q++)\n            p[i * m + q] = q * k + i;\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)\n        result[pos++] = (char)('0' + k - 1);\n\n    for (int i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n        int j = i;\n        while (p[j] >= 0) {\n            result[pos++] = (char)('0' + j / m);\n            int next = p[j];\n            p[j] = -1;\n            j = next;\n        }\n    }\n\n    result[pos] = '\\0';\n    free(p);\n    return result;\n}",
      "C++": "class Solution {\npublic:\n    string crackSafe(int n, int k) {\n        int m = ipow(k, n - 1);\n        int total = m * k;\n        vector<int> p(total);\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)\n            for (int q = 0; q < m; q++)\n                p[i * m + q] = q * k + i;\n\n        string result;\n        result.reserve(total + n - 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++)\n            result.push_back(char('0' + k - 1));\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n            int j = i;\n            while (p[j] >= 0) {\n                result.push_back(char('0' + j / m));\n                int next = p[j];\n                p[j] = -1;\n                j = next;\n            }\n        }\n        return result;\n    }\n};",
      "Java": "class Solution {\n    public String crackSafe(int n, int k) {\n        int m = 1;\n        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) m *= k;\n        int total = m * k;\n\n        int[] p = new int[total];\n        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++)\n            for (int q = 0; q < m; q++)\n                p[i * m + q] = q * k + i;\n\n        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder(total + n - 1);\n        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) result.append((char)('0' + k - 1));\n\n        for (int i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n            int j = i;\n            while (p[j] >= 0) {\n                result.append((char)('0' + j / m));\n                int next = p[j];\n                p[j] = -1;\n                j = next;\n            }\n        }\n        return result.toString();\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "import sys, json\n\nclass Solution:\n    def crackSafe(self, n: int, k: int) -> str:\n        m = k ** (n - 1)\n        p = [q * k + i for i in range(k) for q in range(m)]\n        result = [str(k - 1)] * (n - 1)\n        for i in range(k ** n):\n            j = i\n            while p[j] >= 0:\n                result.append(str(j // m))\n                p[j], j = -1, p[j]\n        return \"\".join(result)",
      "JavaScript": "const readline = require(\"readline\");\n\nfunction crackSafe(n, k) {\n    const m = k ** (n - 1);\n    const total = k ** n;\n    const p = Array.from({ length: total }, (_, index) => {\n        const i = Math.floor(index / m);\n        const q = index % m;\n        return q * k + i;\n    });\n\n    const result = Array(n - 1).fill(String(k - 1));\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < total; i++) {\n        let j = i;\n        while (p[j] >= 0) {\n            result.push(String(Math.floor(j / m)));\n            const next = p[j];\n            p[j] = -1;\n            j = next;\n        }\n    }\n    return result.join(\"\");\n}"
    }
  },
